EXT. SALT FLATS WAY STATION - DAY

White ground. White sky with a seam of heat across it. One adobe hut, roof half gone. A windmill with no blades, only the hub, rusted and ticking.

TUCKER HALE (40s, lean, sun-cracked lips, a hat stained black at the band) sits on an upturned barrel. Rifle across his knees. Not aiming it. Not putting it down.

ISAAC BROOKS (50s, heavy, one eye clouded) crouches by a dead fire, poking ash with a stick. He wears a preacher's coat, two sizes small, buttons mismatched.

Between them, a mule. Grey. Chewing nothing.

                    TUCKER
          Water.

                    ISAAC
          Barrel's dry.

                    TUCKER
          I didn't ask the barrel.

Isaac lifts a canteen. Shakes it. A small sound. He tosses it. Tucker catches it without looking.

                    TUCKER (CONT'D)
          Half.

                    ISAAC
          Drink your half.

Tucker drinks. Caps it. Sets it on the sand between them.

WILHELMINA "BILLIE" STRAND (30s, wiry, a rope-burn scar across one cheek, dust to the knees) steps out of the hut doorway. She carries a tin plate with three strips of jerky. Her boots are two sizes too big. Men's boots.

                    BILLIE
          Supper.

                    TUCKER
          It's noon.

                    BILLIE
          Supper's when you eat it.

She puts the plate down on the barrel top. Takes one strip. Chews. Looks at the horizon.

                    ISAAC
          He's late.

                    TUCKER
          He's always late.

                    BILLIE
          Who's late?

                    TUCKER
          The man with the wagon.

                    BILLIE
          I thought it was a train.

                    ISAAC
          No train. Ground's too soft for rail.

                    BILLIE
          (muttering)
          Everything's too soft for something.

Isaac stands. Knees pop. He walks to the windmill, puts a palm on the post, closes his good eye.

                    ISAAC
          (to himself)
          Lord, I will not ask for rain.
          Lord, I will ask for shade.

Tucker watches him. Says nothing. Billie sits on the hut step, pulls her too-big boots off, empties them. Salt crystals pour out like sugar.

                    BILLIE
          Everybody listen. If we're still here
          at dark, we sleep in shifts.

                    TUCKER
          We won't be here at dark.

                    BILLIE
          Then it costs nothing to agree.

                    ISAAC
          I'll take first.

                    BILLIE
          You'll take middle. You snore on first.

                    ISAAC
          I do not.

                    TUCKER
          You do.

Isaac looks at Tucker. Tucker shrugs.

A mile out, a smudge of dust rises. Not a wagon. Wind, maybe. All three watch it anyway.

                    TUCKER (CONT'D)
          (low, to Isaac)
          You still got the paper?

                    ISAAC
          (low)
          Inside my coat.

                    TUCKER
          Keep it there.

                    BILLIE
          I can hear you.

                    TUCKER
          Then hear this. Nobody does anything
          till I say.

                    BILLIE
          Yes, sir, Marshal.

                    TUCKER
          I'm not a marshal.

                    BILLIE
          That's why it's funny.

The dust smudge thins and dies. Nothing behind it. The mule shifts its weight, sighs through its nose.

Isaac settles back by the dead fire. Pokes. Tucker leans the rifle against the barrel. Billie lies back on the step, hat over her face.

                    BILLIE (CONT'D)
          (from under the hat)
          Wake me when it's supper.

                    TUCKER
          (to the mule)
          She's right, you know.
